= Chemical equilibrium — Technical =

== Thermodynamic criterion ==
At fixed temperature and pressure, reaction equilibrium is associated with vanishing reaction Gibbs-energy change for an unconstrained reaction coordinate at an interior equilibrium:

<pre>
Δ_r G = Δ_r G° + RT ln Q
At equilibrium: Q = K and Δ_r G = 0
K = exp[-Δ_r G°/(RT)]
</pre>

Q must be dimensionless and built from species activities relative to suitable standard states. Ideal-gas partial-pressure or dilute-solution concentration expressions are approximations under specified conditions; pure solids and liquids in standard states contribute unit activity to common equilibrium expressions.

== Reversible kinetics ==
For a simple elementary reversible step at equilibrium, the forward and reverse rates balance. More complex networks can involve multiple coupled reactions, conservation constraints, nonideality, and kinetically persistent nonequilibrium states. A constant observed macroscopic quantity does not alone prove equilibrium.

== Perturbations ==
Changing temperature changes the equilibrium constant according to thermodynamic relations. Changing initial amounts at fixed temperature changes the reaction quotient; the system can evolve until Q equals K if kinetics and constraints permit. A catalyst changes kinetics but not the standard Gibbs-energy difference or K at the same temperature.

== Interpretation ==
Chemical equilibrium is not synonymous with biological [[Feedback|feedback]], ecological steady state, or any time-invariant measurement. Precise use requires a specified reaction, state variables, constraints and reference conditions.

